{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'for f in /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1315637744/11/*.py; do [ -f \"$f\" ] && echo \"FILE:$f\" && sed -n '\"'1,220p' \\\"\"'$f\" | sed -n '\"'/\"'^def main/,/''^def /p;/''^def generate_input/,/''^def /p'\"'; done\"","aggregated_output":"FILE:/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1315637744/11/2.py\ndef main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"\n    Recolor the grey path by corner markers and add same-color cross arms at those markers.\n\n    Input structure:\n    - The background is black.\n    - One connected one-pixel-wide orthogonal path runs from one endpoint to another.\n    - Most path pixels are grey; some corner pixels are replaced by unique non-grey marker colors.\n\n    Rule:\n    - Recover the path order and compare the two endpoint-side prefixes before the first marker; walk from the endpoint with the longer prefix.\n    - Keep both non-corner endpoints grey.\n    - As the walk proceeds, each encountered marker color becomes the active color, and every later grey non-endpoint path pixel keeps that color until a new marker is reached.\n    - At every colored corner, extend the marker color by two pixels along the two directions not already occupied by the path.\n\n    Key insight:\n    - The colored corner pixels act as ordered paint switches on a single polyline, and each marked corner also reveals where the missing two arms of a local cross must be added.\n    \"\"\"\n    info = _analyze_path(np.asarray(grid, dtype=int))\n    g = info[\"grid\"]\n    h, w = g.shape\n    out = g.copy()\n\n    order = _choose_walk_order(info)\n    endpoints = {order[0], order[-1]}\n    active_color = None\n    for pos in order:\n        if pos in endpoints:\n            continue\n        if pos in info[\"marked_corners\"]:\n            active_color = int(info[\"marked_corners\"][pos])\n            continue\n        if int(g[pos]) == info[\"path_color\"] and active_color is not None:\n            out[pos] = int(active_color)\n\n    for corner, color in info[\"marked_corners\"].items():\n        for rr, cc in _extension_cells(corner, info[\"corner_dirs\"][corner]):\n            assert 0 <= rr < h and 0 <= cc < w, \"cross extension must stay inside the grid\"\n            out[rr, cc] = int(color)\n\n    return out\n\n\ndef _vertices_to_order(vertices: list[tuple[int, int]]) -> list[tuple[int, int]]:\nFILE:/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1315637744/11/3.py\ndef main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"\n    Replace each seed-filled grey enclosure with the concentric-ring template indicated by its seed count.\n\n    Input structure:\n    - Black background with several one-pixel-thick grey rectangular fences.\n    - Each task fence encloses only black cells plus 1 to 4 red seed pixels.\n    - A teaching area sits near the lower-right corner and shows the four ring templates for counts 1..4.\n\n    Rule:\n    - Find every grey hollow rectangle whose interior contains only black and red.\n    - Count the red seeds inside that rectangle, clear those seeds, and choose the matching 1..4 layer ring template.\n    - Center the chosen template inside the rectangle's interior, leaving any extra interior padding black.\n    - Keep the grey fences and the teaching area unchanged.\n\n    Key insight:\n    The bottom-right teaching examples define a fixed count-to-template mapping, so each fenced region is solved independently by counting its red seeds and stamping the corresponding centered ring.\n    \"\"\"\n    g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)\n    out = g.copy()\n    for r0, c0, r1, c1 in _find_rect_frames(g):\n        interior = g[r0 + 1 : r1, c0 + 1 : c1]\n        colors = set(map(int, np.unique(interior)))\n        if not colors.issubset({BG, RED}):\n            continue\n        seed_count = int(np.count_nonzero(interior == RED))\n        if 1 <= seed_count <= 4:\n            _paint_centered_ring(out, r0, c0, r1, c1, seed_count)\n    return out\n\n\ndef _draw_task_frame(\nFILE:/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1315637744/11/4.py\ndef main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"\n    Transform each marked monochrome shape in place according to the maroon control pixels on its boundary.\n\n    Input structure:\n    - The background is black.\n    - Each foreground object is a monochrome shape together with one or two maroon marker pixels attached on the shape boundary.\n    - Different shapes are disconnected from one another, and each object's tight bounding box is the frame used for its transformation.\n\n    Rule:\n    - For each connected object, treat maroon pixels as control markers rather than body color.\n    - A top-middle marker means vertical flip, a right-middle marker means clockwise 90-degree rotation, a bottom-middle marker means no geometric change, a left-middle marker means counterclockwise 90-degree rotation.\n    - Two markers on top-left and bottom-right mean 180-degree rotation; two markers on bottom-left and top-right mean horizontal flip.\n    - Replace marker pixels with the object's own color, apply the indicated transform about the bounding-box center, and redraw the transformed shape back into the same bounding box.\n\n    Key insight:\n    - The maroon pixels encode an operation by where they sit on the object's boundary, so solving the task means separating signal pixels from the true body before transforming the whole bbox patch.\n    \"\"\"\n    g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)\n    out = g.copy()\n\n    for component in _find_components(g != BG_COLOR):\n        r0, c0, r1, c1 = map(int, component[\"bbox\"])\n        h = int(r1 - r0 + 1)\n        w = int(c1 - c0 + 1)\n        patch = np.full((h, w), BG_COLOR, dtype=int)\n\n        colors = {int(g[r, c]) for r, c in component[\"coords\"]}\n        assert MARKER_COLOR in colors, \"each shape must contain maroon control markers\"\n        body_colors = colors - {MARKER_COLOR}\n        assert len(body_colors) == 1, \"each shape body must be monochrome\"\n        body_color = int(next(iter(body_colors)))\n\n        marker_coords: list[tuple[int, int]] = []\n        for r, c in component[\"coords\"]:\n            rr = int(r - r0)\n            cc = int(c - c0)\n            if int(g[r, c]) == MARKER_COLOR:\n                marker_coords.append((rr, cc))\n            patch[rr, cc] = body_color\n\n        assert 1 <= len(marker_coords) <= 2, \"expected one or two markers per object\"\n        transform = _infer_transform(marker_coords, patch.shape)\n        transformed = _apply_transform(patch, transform)\n        assert transformed.shape == patch.shape, \"transformed patch must stay in its bbox\"\n\n        region = out[r0 : r1 + 1, c0 : c1 + 1]\n        region[:] = BG_COLOR\n        region[transformed != BG_COLOR] = body_color\n\n    return out\n\n\ndef _touches_all_sides(mask: np.ndarray) -> bool:\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
