{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1315637744/11/codegen_prompts/5.md\"","aggregated_output":"你要为一个新的 ARC-AGI 风格题目实现代码。代码文件必须包含 `main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray` 和 `generate_input() -> np.ndarray`。\n\n## 新题位置\n\n- 描述目录：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1315637744/11`\n- 题号：`5`\n- 目标代码路径：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1315637744/11/5.py`\n\n## 新题描述\n\n<new_puzzle index=\"5\">\n- `description`：沿管道网络传播染料，在交叉口根据管道宽度决定染料走向，窄管优先。\n- `Input structure`：背景为黑色。网格中有由 grey 像素构成的正交管道网络：管道是水平或垂直的矩形通道，宽度为 1 到 3 个像素不等。管道在交叉口处相交。网络的某一个末端管口处有一个彩色像素（如 red），作为染料源。\n- `Rule`：\n  - 从染料源开始，沿管道方向将所有 grey 像素染为染料颜色。\n  - 当染料到达一个交叉口时，交叉口本身被染色，然后染料只进入该交叉口连接的所有管道中宽度最窄的那一条继续传播（不回头进入来源管道）。\n  - 如果有多条管道宽度相同且都是最窄的，则染料同时进入所有这些最窄管道。\n  - 染料不进入的管道保持 grey。\n  - 染料传播直到到达管道末端（死胡同）或网格边界。\n- `Key insight`：解题者需要先识别管道网络的拓扑结构，再发现交叉口处的选择规则不是\"全部扩散\"而是\"选择最窄的管道\"。管道宽度是肉眼可直接判断的视觉属性，但将其作为路径选择的判据是需要归纳的非显然规则。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n## 机制相似参考题\n\n下面参考题由本地相似度流程自动选择：先用 `annotations`/docstring/代码特征抽取机制标签，再按机制相似度、文本相似度和代码特征相似度重排，并用 MMR 做去重。优先学习这些题的对象识别方式、随机生成器约束、边界条件处理，不要照搬旧题规则。\n\n已选参考：eval/332f06d7, train/538b439f, train/78e78cff, train/d93c6891, train/278e5215, eval/dbff022c, eval/9385bd28, eval/8b7bacbf\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"332f06d7\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3201\nmechanism=0.3647\nlexical=0.0175\ncode_feature=0.5714\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, code:counter, object:path, operation:count, operation:flood_fill, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/332f06d7.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/332f06d7.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nSlide a solid square through a winding channel to reach a same-sized target square.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg (most common color). A river color forms a winding corridor.\n- Two same-sized NxN solid squares on the river: mover and destination, each a distinct color.\n\nRule:\n- The mover square travels along the river toward the destination.\n- It can only pass through positions where the full NxN area is river.\n- If reachable, the mover replaces the destination; otherwise it stops at the farthest reachable point.\n- The mover's original position is filled with the river color.\n\nKey insight: Chokepoints narrower than N pixels block the square, forcing it to stop short.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_along_path\",\n  \"src\": \"black square\",\n  \"path\": \"blue pixels \\\"water channel\\\" that is wide enough to pass through until \\\"stuck\\\" because of too narrow\",\n  \"dst\": \"reach same sized red square or \\\"get stuck\\\" on the way where the blue path is too narrow\",\n  \"transform\": \"cover the red square if reaching it\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"538b439f\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3046\nmechanism=0.3555\nlexical=0.0142\ncode_feature=0.5000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, object:path, object:rectangle, operation:count, operation:recolor, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/538b439f.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/538b439f.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nReflect the solid rectangular source objects across the full separator line and color the gap with the separator color.\n\nInput structure:\n- A dominant background contains one full-width or full-height one-pixel\n  separator line in a non-background color.\n- One other foreground color forms solid rectangular objects, all strictly on the same side of that separator.\n- Remaining non-background pixels are scattered distractors.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the input grid shape and unchanged pixels outside each affected corridor.\n- Mirror each source object across the separator line.\n- For every source object, recolor the rectangular strip between the\n  original object and its reflection to the separator color, then paint the\n  reflected object in the source color.\n\nKey insight:\n- The full straight line is a mirror axis; the real objects are identified\n  structurally as the only solid rectangles confined to one side, while\n  scattered pixels are noise that may be overwritten inside the corridor.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"symmetry/pattern\",\n  \"src\": \"solid rectangular foreground objects all on one side of the full separator row/column\",\n  \"dst\": \"their mirror positions across the separator line\",\n  \"transform\": \"copy each source object by reflection; recolor the rectangular strip between each source object and its mirror to the separator color, overwriting background or distractor pixels inside that strip\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"78e78cff\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.2930\nmechanism=0.3973\nlexical=0.0144\ncode_feature=0.1667\noverlap_tags=code:connected_components, object:grid_edge, operation:count, operation:flood_fill, operation:recolor, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/78e78cff.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/78e78cff.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nRecolor the row-wise passage indicated by wall pixels with the singleton marker color.\n\nInput structure:\n- A dominant background contains one singleton marker pixel and one wall color.\n- Wall pixels lie on several rows and form left/right limits around the marker column.\n- Empty rows between the top and bottom wall rows are open horizontal passages.\n\nRule:\n- On a wall row, paint background pixels between the nearest wall strictly\n  left of the marker column and the nearest wall at or right of it; if only\n  one side is present, paint from that wall to the opposite grid edge.\n- On empty rows between the top and bottom wall rows, paint the whole row.\n- On empty rows outside the wall-row band, reuse the nearest end wall-row interval.\n- Preserve wall pixels and the original marker pixel.\n\nKey insight:\n- The wall color defines scanline limits relative to the marker column; the\n  marker color fills those implied horizontal spans rather than flood-filling\n  through connected background.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src\": \"one singleton marker pixel and rows of wall-color pixels on a dominant background\",\n  \"transform\": \"recolor background pixels in the scanline passage selected by the marker column: wall rows fill between nearest wall limits, empty interior rows fill full width, and empty exterior rows reuse the nearest end interval\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"d93c6891\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.2988\nmechanism=0.3469\nlexical=0.0144\ncode_feature=0.5000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, object:grid_edge, object:rectangle, operation:count, operation:recolor, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/d93c6891.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/d93c6891.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nMove each gray bar into its adjacent orange rectangle as full stripes from the contacted edge.\n\nInput structure\n- The scene uses black outside space, yellow fill, gray one-cell-thick bars, and solid orange rectangles.\n- Each gray bar 4-touches exactly one orange rectangle; some orange rectangles have no touching gray bar.\n- Gray bars touching the same orange rectangle all have the same orientation.\n\nRule\n- Recolor every original gray cell to yellow.\n- For each orange rectangle touched by gray bars, count the gray cells assigned to that rectangle.\n- Horizontal gray bars repaint that many cells as complete orange rows starting from the contacted top or bottom row.\n- Vertical gray bars repaint that many cells as complete orange columns starting from the contacted left or right column.\n- Orange rectangles with no touching gray bar remain unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The gray pixels are not copied by shape; their count is transferred into the neighboring orange rectangle as edge-aligned row or column stripes.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"gray one-cell-thick bars s\",\n  \"dst\": \"4-adjacent solid orange rectangles d\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"s 4-touches d\",\n  \"transform\": \"erase s to yellow; in each matched d, repaint |matched s cells| orange cells gray as complete rows for horizontal bars or complete columns for vertical bars from the touched edge\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"278e5215\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.2848\nmechanism=0.2959\nlexical=0.0180\ncode_feature=0.6667\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, code:counter, object:rectangle, operation:count, operation:recolor, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/278e5215.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/278e5215.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nRecolor the large mask crop column-by-column using the separate three-row palette strip.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the most common color.\n- One large monochrome foreground object is the mask; its bbox is the output region and may contain background holes.\n- The remaining foreground is a 3-row palette rectangle with the same width as the mask bbox: two identical color rows and one uniform row.\n\nRule:\n- Crop to the mask object's bbox.\n- For each output column, color mask pixels with the corresponding color from the repeated palette rows.\n- Replace background holes inside the crop with the palette's uniform third-row color.\n\nKey insight:\nThe grey object is only a stencil; its columns select colors from the legend, while its holes expose the legend's fill color.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"palette column s in the separate 3-row strip\",\n  \"dst\": \"column d in the mask bbox crop\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"s.column == d.column_in_crop\",\n  \"transform\": \"recolor mask pixels in d with s.repeated_row_color; fill background holes with the palette's uniform third-row color\",\n  \"symbol_instructions\": \"3-row palette strip maps each mask-crop column to its mask color; the uniform third row gives the hole color\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"crop to mask bbox\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"dbff022c\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.2609\nmechanism=0.2915\nlexical=0.0137\ncode_feature=0.5000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, object:grid_edge, object:path, operation:flood_fill, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/dbff022c.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/dbff022c.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nFill hollow sprites' interiors using a color-palette legend on the grid edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Several hollow sprites with distinct border colors.\n- A small 2×N or N×2 palette block sits on one grid edge; one row/column lists border colors, the other lists corresponding fill colors.\n\nRule:\n- Locate the palette legend on a grid edge and read it as a border→fill color mapping.\n- Find enclosed bg regions (holes) inside sprites that don't touch the grid boundary.\n- For each hole, identify the most common adjacent border color, look it up in the palette, and fill the hole with the mapped fill color.\n\nKey insight: The palette's edge position and orientation determine reading direction; border colors in the palette uniquely key into fill colors.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
